  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 PM EDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nA MCS has again developed over central IA this morning and has\nbeen making its way eastward. The stronger convective\ncirculation has been pushing southeastward along the stubborn\nquasi-stationary boundary that has hung around the area the last\nseveral days and current radar imagery shows the stronger \nstorms pushing just south of our CWA along this boundary over \nthe next couple of hours. The rest of the area will see a weaker\nconvective band with light to moderate rainfall push in from the\nwest with a few embedded thunderstorms possible but storms are\nexpected to remain weak with the better instability and moisture\nfurther south. \n\nLater tonight the main concern becomes the potential for heavy\nrainfall with the boundary slightly shifting northward and \nbecome orientated northwest to southeast across the center of \nthe CWA once again. A northward surge of moisture and forcing \nalong the boundary will envelop the area and bring a resurgence \nof a band of convective thunderstorms and rainfall across the \narea. With the general westerly to easterly flow training of \ncells will bring moderate to heavy rainfall for areas directly \nunderneath where the band sets up and this includes areas where \nrecent rains in the previous days have made soils quite moist. \nCurrent guidance does show this band setting up from the US-30 \ncorridor and southward however the models have had a horrendous \ntime with this \"ring of fire\" pattern in place and is likely to \ncontinue to struggle with such a stagnant pattern in place. \nTherefore, confidence is low in the exact placement of the band \nbut does seem to make sense that the band will set up along the \nboundary and more northern areas are more stable. \n\nStarting to sound like a broken record but for the longer term\nthere is still considerable uncertainty with this very stagnant\npattern that models struggle resolving since this is not a\nnormal pattern and the models tend to be more progressive which\nis the norm. But all indications look to be a continued zonal\npattern with periods of showers and thunderstorms parading over\nthe area, however there is some hints into the ridge over the \nsouth trying to push northward this weekend which may push the \nrainfall activity further northward which would be welcome as \nour southern parts of our CWA and areas over IND CWA have been \ndrenched and can hardly take any more.",
